cff-version: 1.2.0
message: 'If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata.'
title: 'IELTS API: a free, no-authentication REST API and open dataset for IELTS preparation research'
type: software
version: 1.2.0
date-released: '2026-09-05'
abstract: >-
  IELTS API is an open, dependency-free TypeScript REST API that exposes
  machine-readable IELTS preparation data: 4,174 headwords extracted from the
  Cambridge IELTS volumes 1-22 vocabulary lists, condensed analytic band
  descriptors for Speaking and Writing across bands 0-9, indicative score
  concordances for CEFR, TOEFL iBT, the Cambridge English Scale, PTE Academic
  and the Duolingo English Test, Writing and Speaking task banks, a canonical
  taxonomy of the thirteen IELTS Reading and Listening question types with the
  frequencies observed in 27,225 practice questions, a structure and
  readability index of 1,702 practice tests and CEFR-graded reading lessons,
  fifty recurring exam themes, and a curated index of an open IELTS research
  corpus. Beyond serving datasets, the API measures submitted texts:
  /v1/analyze/text returns deterministic counts, six readability formulae
  (Flesch, Flesch-Kincaid, Gunning Fog, SMOG, Coleman-Liau, Automated
  Readability Index), lexical-diversity measures including MTLD, a documented
  grade-to-CEFR heuristic with an indicative band range, and a
  vocabulary-coverage profile against the Cambridge headword list, while
  /v1/analyze/reference publishes every formula, threshold and definition so a
  study can cite the metric, not just the number. Every endpoint is free of
  charge,
  requires no authentication, returns a uniform JSON envelope with a stable
  ETag, and is covered by a test suite with 100% statement, branch, function
  and line coverage. The API is designed for reproducible second-language
  assessment research: responses are deterministic, versioned and citable.
keywords:
  - IELTS
  - English for Academic Purposes
  - second language assessment
  - vocabulary
  - band descriptors
  - score concordance
  - question types
  - readability
  - Flesch Reading Ease
  - CEFR
  - open data
  - REST API
  - TypeScript
